# Build contexts are whole-repo now: the Dockerfiles hand the workspace to
# cargo-chef (`COPY . .`) instead of listing crate manifests one by one, so
# what lands in the image is decided here rather than by a COPY allow-list.
target/
target-wt/
.git/
.github/
.githooks/
.cline/
docs/
infra/
crates/overslash-api/tests/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
dashboard/.svelte-kit/
dashboard/test-results/
dashboard/playwright-report/
*.md
!crates/**/README.md
!SKILL.md
.env
.env.*
docker-compose*.yml
Makefile
SCHEMA.sql

# The toolchain inside an image comes from the `FROM rust:<ver>` tag, not from
# here. Shipping the toml would make rustup sync a *second* toolchain: we pin a
# `<major>.<minor>` channel and the base image ships an exact patch release
# (`1.98` vs `1.98.1`), and rustup treats those as unrelated toolchain names
# rather than normalising one onto the other. That cost 1.5G and ~15s in the
# `planner` and `builder` stages of every build — both sit after `COPY . .`, so
# no layer cache could ever absorb it.
# scripts/check-rust-version.sh keeps the FROM tags and the toml in step.
rust-toolchain.toml
